^SERIES = GSE190604
!Series_title = CRISPR activation and interference screens decode stimulation responses in primary human T cells [CRISPRa Perturb-seq]
!Series_geo_accession = GSE190604
!Series_status = Public on Feb 04 2022
!Series_submission_date = Dec 09 2021
!Series_last_update_date = Feb 07 2022
!Series_pubmed_id = 35113687
!Series_summary = Regulation of cytokine production in stimulated T cells can be disrupted in autoimmunity, immunodeficiencies, and cancer. Systematic discovery of stimulation-dependent cytokine regulators requires both loss-of-function and gain-of-function studies, which have been challenging in primary human cells. We now report genome-wide CRISPR activation (CRISPRa) and interference (CRISPRi) screens in primary human T cells to identify gene networks controlling interleukin 2 and interferon gamma production. Arrayed CRISPRa confirmed key hits and enabled multiplexed secretome characterization, revealing reshaped cytokine responses. Coupling CRISPRa screening with single-cell RNA-seq enabled deep molecular characterization of screen hits, revealing how perturbations tuned T cell activation and promoted cell states characterized by distinct cytokine expression profiles. Together, these screens reveal genes that reprogram critical immune cell functions, which could inform the design of immunotherapies.
!Series_overall_design = Pooled CRISPRa screen coupled with single-cell RNA-seq in human T cells
!Series_type = Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing
!Series_type = Other
